\name{multiScaleR-glossary}
\alias{multiScaleR-glossary}
\title{Glossary of terms used in multiScaleR}
\description{
Plain-language definitions of the terms used across the multiScaleR help
pages and vignettes, in alphabetical order. Each entry names the page that
explains the idea in more depth.
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item \strong{1D and 2D distance}: two ways to turn sigma into a distance. The 2D
distance (the default) is the radius of the circle around a point that
holds a set proportion of an ideal kernel's weight on a plane. The 1D
distance is measured along a line through the point and is shorter. See
\code{\link[=kernel_dist]{kernel_dist()}}.
\item \strong{Backend}: the computational route \code{\link[=multiScale_optim]{multiScale_optim()}} uses to search
over scales, chosen with its \code{backend} argument. See
\link{multiScaleR-fitting-methods}.
\item \strong{Binned inputs}: distance-binned summaries that \code{\link[=kernel_prep]{kernel_prep()}} stores
when \code{bin = TRUE} (the default). They let the search compute
kernel-weighted means, and kernel-weighted surface metrics other than
\code{"sa"}, without visiting every cell, which is much faster.
\item \strong{Buffer}: the set of raster cells within \code{max_D} of a survey point. Only
these cells contribute to the point's kernel-weighted covariates.
\item \strong{Choice set}: in a step-selection model, one used step together with
the available steps it is compared against. See
\code{vignette("v10-step-selection", package = "multiScaleR")}.
\item \strong{Conditional uncertainty}: uncertainty that treats the estimated scales
as known. Most coefficient standard errors and prediction intervals are
conditional. See \link{multiScaleR-prediction}.
\item \strong{Credible interval}: a Bayesian interval that contains a stated
posterior probability (for example 95 percent) for a quantity, given the
model, the data, and the priors. See \code{\link[=multiScale_bayes]{multiScale_bayes()}}.
\item \strong{Cross-validation}: estimating out-of-sample predictive performance by
fitting the model to part of the data (the training folds) and scoring its
predictions on the rest (the held-out fold). \emph{Spatial folds} hold out
blocks of nearby points, and \emph{grouped folds} hold out whole groups such as
sites or years. See \code{\link[=cross_validate]{cross_validate()}}.
\item \strong{Distance-removal model}: a model for counts recorded in distance
classes and removal intervals that separates abundance from detection.
multiScaleR uses \code{unmarked}'s generalized distance-removal (GDR) models.
See \code{vignette("v12-distance-removal", package = "multiScaleR")}.
\item \strong{Effective distance}: the distance that contains a set proportion
(by default 90 percent) of a kernel's weight. It converts sigma into a
distance in map units that is easy to report and compare across kernels.
See \code{\link[=kernel_dist]{kernel_dist()}}.
\item \strong{Expected response}: the mean response a model predicts for given
covariate values, such as an expected count or an occurrence probability.
It differs from an \emph{observation replicate}, a simulated new observation
that also includes observation noise. See \link{multiScaleR-prediction}.
\item \strong{Exponential power kernel} (\code{"expow"}): a kernel with a shape parameter
in addition to sigma, which lets the weight curve range from sharply
peaked to nearly flat-topped. See \link{multiScaleR-kernels}.
\item \strong{Fixed kernel}: a kernel that gives every cell within a radius the same
weight and cells beyond it no weight. Its scale is a hard radius.
\item \strong{Fixed-radius metric}: a landscape or surface metric computed at a
radius you set, so it has no scale to estimate and enters the model as an
ordinary covariate. See \code{\link[=msr_vars]{msr_vars()}}.
\item \strong{Gaussian kernel}: the default kernel, whose weights decline with
distance following a normal (bell-shaped) curve with standard deviation
sigma. See \link{multiScaleR-kernels}.
\item \strong{Hard radius}: a scale at which a cell is either fully in or fully out
of the calculation. Landscape metrics, unweighted surface metrics, and the
fixed kernel use hard radii. Their likelihood changes in steps, so they get
no Hessian standard error.
\item \strong{Hessian}: the matrix of second derivatives of the log-likelihood at its
maximum. Its inverse approximates the variance of the estimates and gives
Wald standard errors.
\item \strong{Joint uncertainty}: uncertainty that lets the scales and the
coefficients vary together, so that intervals also reflect uncertainty
about the scales. Only some backends provide it. See
\link{multiScaleR-fitting-methods}.
\item \strong{Kernel}: a function that assigns a weight to each raster cell
according to its distance from a survey point. Kernel-weighted covariates
summarize the landscape around each point. See \link{multiScaleR-kernels}.
\item \strong{Kernel-weighted mean}: the weighted average of a raster's cell values
around a point, using kernel weights. It is the default covariate type,
set with \code{\link[=kernel_var]{kernel_var()}}.
\item \strong{Landscape metric}: a summary of the composition or arrangement of
land-cover classes within a radius, such as the proportion of a class or
an edge density, set with \code{\link[=landscape_var]{landscape_var()}}.
\item \strong{Laplace approximation}: a fast approximation that treats a
distribution (a posterior, or the integral over random effects) as
Gaussian around its peak. See
\code{vignette("v15-bayesian-computation", package = "multiScaleR")}.
\item \strong{Lean mode}: preparing inputs with \code{store_cell_data = FALSE}, which
keeps only the binned summaries to save memory. It works only when every
covariate is a kernel-weighted mean.
\item \strong{Link scale and response scale}: a generalized linear model works on
the link scale (for example log counts or log odds) and reports
predictions on the response scale (counts or probabilities) after
back-transforming with the inverse link. See \link{multiScaleR-prediction}.
\item \strong{Marginal likelihood}: the likelihood after integrating out random
quantities, such as random effects or a between-group standard deviation
of scale.
\item \strong{\code{max_D} and \code{min_D}}: the largest and smallest scales the search may
consider, in map units. Set \code{max_D} in \code{\link[=kernel_prep]{kernel_prep()}} above the largest
plausible scale of effect; \code{min_D} is set from the raster resolution.
\item \strong{Negative exponential kernel} (\code{"exp"}): a kernel whose weights decline
exponentially with distance, giving more weight to nearby cells and a
longer tail than the Gaussian kernel. See \link{multiScaleR-kernels}.
\item \strong{Posterior}: in a Bayesian model, the probability distribution of the
parameters given the data and the priors. See \code{\link[=multiScale_bayes]{multiScale_bayes()}}.
\item \strong{Profile likelihood}: the log-likelihood as a function of one parameter,
with every other parameter re-estimated at each value. Profile-likelihood
intervals follow the shape of the likelihood instead of assuming it is
symmetric. See \code{\link[=profile_sigma]{profile_sigma()}}.
\item \strong{Scale of effect}: the distance over which a landscape variable
influences the response. multiScaleR estimates it as the sigma of a
kernel, which \code{\link[=kernel_dist]{kernel_dist()}} converts to an effective distance.
\item \strong{Shape}: the second parameter of the exponential power kernel, which
controls how sharply the weights fall off.
\item \strong{Sigma}: the scale parameter of a kernel, in map units. A larger sigma
spreads the weight over a wider area. Sigma is not itself the distance
to report; see \emph{Effective distance}.
\item \strong{Spatial response field}: a spatially correlated random effect that
captures residual spatial pattern the covariates leave unexplained. See
\code{vignette("v11-spatial-fields", package = "multiScaleR")}.
\item \strong{SQ}: the kernel-weighted root-mean-square deviation of a continuous
surface around its local mean, a measure of surface roughness. See
\code{\link[=surface_var]{surface_var()}}.
\item \strong{Standardization}: centering a covariate on its mean and dividing by its
standard deviation. multiScaleR standardizes each optimized covariate at
every candidate scale by default, so a coefficient describes the effect of
a one standard deviation change.
\item \strong{Step-selection model}: a model of animal movement that compares each
used step with available steps through conditional logistic regression.
See \code{vignette("v10-step-selection", package = "multiScaleR")}.
\item \strong{Surface metric}: a summary of the texture of a continuous surface
around a point, such as its roughness or skewness, set with
\code{\link[=surface_var]{surface_var()}}.
\item \strong{Wald interval}: an interval built from an estimate plus or minus a
multiple of its standard error. It assumes a symmetric, nearly quadratic
likelihood near the estimate, which may not hold for scales.
}
}
